 #:seon.task{:name "one-test-check-takes-74-seconds"
             :plan "`seon.operator/check!` (`bin/seon check --test NS/NAME`) took 74-95 s per run in fan-out 9 (2026-10-09, 995b01c2d) for a handful of named tests whose bodies run in milliseconds; a throwaway JVM is ready in about 10 s with cached classes (`bin/seon start --head` ready-ms 10158). The cost must follow the tests requested: a check of one test costs JVM readiness plus loading the namespaces that test reaches plus the test, not whole-program publication, adoption rehearsal, full lint or whole-program load. Measure each phase of `seon.operator/check!` first, then remove or narrow every phase not proportional to the requested tests. The same path serves the platform test step (an agent's host-bound change runs its affected tests in a throwaway JVM), so its floor sets how fast platform work can iterate. Read `seon.operator/operator-check-bound-ms` and `docs/seon/schedule/cards/PLATFORM-STEP.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "check-classpath-build-races-in-tools-deps"
             :plan "bin/seon check sometimes fails at `clojure -Spath -M:dev:test` with 'Error building classpath. class java.util.HashMap$Node cannot be cast to class java.util.HashMap$TreeNode' from `org.apache.maven.model.validation.DefaultModelValidator/validateId` under `clojure.tools.deps/expand-deps` (a HashMap shared across its parallel children tasks); the identical retry passes. Seen twice on 2026-10-10 (lane loop-impl-cost; operator-check-20261010T094742.914, operator-check-20261010T095938.248). Done when the check's classpath step cannot fail this way (a cached classpath per deps digest, or a serialized expansion), with the retry no longer needed. Read `seon.operator/request!`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "bin-lane-claim-refuses-the-lanes-own-edit-and-any-new-file"
             :plan "`bin/lane claim` refuses a path the same lane edited before claiming it, and any untracked new file, as someone else's uncommitted change (loop-prove and loop-impl-flow, 86b7cdbbe and aca57cc39, 2026-10-10), so lanes moved files aside, claimed, and moved them back. The rule that protects another lane's work in progress (claim refuses a dirty unclaimed path, bin/lane header lines 52-54) has no way to tell a lane's own edit from a foreign one. Done when claim accepts a dirty path whose only writer is the claiming lane (its released.holders record, or the dirty digest it last saw) and an untracked file no other lane holds, still refusing a path another lane claimed or edited, with a regression in test/seon/operator_test.clj style on a scratch repository. Read `seon.operator/check!` and `bin/lane`."}
