 #:seon.task{:name "an-extracted-archive-is-read-only"
             :plan "A committed archive data/source/<sha> (`seon.operator/committed-source!`) is the running JVM's program and working directory, yet an agent's relative `(spit \"src/seon/sci/forgive.clj\" ...)` rewrote the running archive's platform source (observed 2026-10-10, chain run 2), to be loaded by the next reload or by a restart reusing the sha. Done when `seon.operator/seal-archive!` clears the write bits of every committed regular file of the archive before it is moved into place, deleters (`prune-archives!`, nuke) still delete it whole, and a committed source file refuses a write. The same holds for the gitlink pins the archive links (`reference-code/*` resolves to data/source/pins/..., which a JVM loads Datahike, SCI and Malli from): `seon.operator/seal-pinned!` seals the files git tracks at each pin (nested gitlinks included) after tools.deps preparation, leaving untracked build products writable, and pruning still deletes pins whole. Read `seon.operator/prune-archives!`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-booting-jvms-class-directory-is-pruned-under-it"
  :plan "`bin/seon start --head` onto af605e344 (2026-10-10 17:37) failed: the new JVM died with `java.nio.file.NoSuchFileException` for target/dev-program-classes/041748f9.../seon/program$entity_identities.class, and the directory was gone afterwards, while lane test runs (`bin/seon check`) sharing the linked `target` ran concurrently. The retry booted. `seon.operator/prune-archives!` and the program-class pruning protect a class directory a live process names (`seon.operator-move-test/a-class-directory-a-live-process-names-is-never-pruned`), but a replacement JVM that has not yet advertised itself is not live. Correct: a class directory selected for a boot that has not finished is held from its selection until the boot exits or advertises; the failed start's diagnostic names the pruner. Its rollback then reported `:rollback :unknown` with no captured heads, which reads as data loss though none occurred: state that nothing was captured because the replacement died first. Read `seon.operator/request!` and `docs/seon/plan.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-jvm-runs-in-a-run-directory-not-its-archive"
  :plan "Fences outside the night (no-fences law): `seon.operator/seal-archive!` (9cd874d94) and `seon.operator/seal-pinned!` (4027882d4) clear write bits on the committed archive and pinned checkouts because an agent's relative `spit` rewrote the running archive (observed), yet a sibling write plus rename still replaces a sealed file. Root design: the JVM's working directory is a per-run directory the operator creates, never the archive, and the archive and pins are read only through the classpath; a relative write then lands in the run directory. Done when both sealing functions are deleted and an agent's relative `spit` leaves the archive unchanged."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-test-launched-jvm-exits-when-its-launcher-exits"
  :plan "A JVM launched for a test or a check outlives its launcher. On 2026-10-11 root found nine JVMs still running with parent pid 1, started 2026-10-08..10: roots data/check/<sha>-<pid>/.../tmp/b1b-* (children of `bin/seon check` throwaway JVMs) and data/source/871e6a979.../tmp/b1b-* (children of default while it ran 871e6a979), each a scratch root of `seon.cluster.boot-test` drills. `seon.operator/launch-child!` starts every JVM as a detached daemon, as `start` needs for default, so when the launching test JVM is replaced, stopped or killed at its bound before the drill's cleanup runs, nothing ends the child; root had to kill them by hand. Reproduced the same day by lane revert-verify: a full compiled tier on an isolated root (tmp/test-runs/revert-baseline-*) left boot-test drill JVMs under its data/source/<sha>/tmp/b1b-* with parent pid 1 while their test members ran past the request's 240 s check limit. Done when a JVM launched under a test or check root (`seon.operator/launched-throwaway!`, the drills' `bin/seon --root ... start`) exits when its launcher process exits (the launcher's pid observed through `java.lang.ProcessHandle/onExit`, decided at launch from the request, never by a sweep), while default still outlives `bin/seon start`; proven by a drill that kills a launcher and observes its child's exit within the bound. Read `seon.operator/check!`."}
