 #:seon.task{:name "a-check-waits-for-a-slot-longer-than-it-runs"
             :plan "Commits on 2026-10-14 waited 193 s of a 259 s check (s1c-1), 185 s of 236 s (check-class-race), 119 s of 179 s (lane-cutoff-heads), 70 s of 115 s (lane-retention-off) in `seon.operator/check-slot!`'s queue with two slots, and loaded checks flaked tests against their bounds. Raising `:seon.config.operator/check-slots` from 2 to 4 (owner 2026-10-14: more load is fine, but the wait is a problem to solve, not to hide) buys time only. The work a check does is a whole JVM boot per check (default's ready time is 35-39 s) plus publication and reload of the candidate before the named tests run. Done when a check's wall time is proportional to the tests it names and the files it changes: measure where a check's time goes (boot layers, publication, the named tests) on three real commits, then remove the work that does not depend on the change (e.g. reuse a booted check JVM per archive, or publish only the changed namespaces), with `seon.profile/measure` evidence and the queue wait per commit recorded before and after."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-quoted-multi-path-variable-is-refused-naming-its-argument"
